{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB06768 - MONDO_5480",
  "verbalization": "Ammonium Lactate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Contact Dermatitis through a multi-step mechanism involving inflammatory and immune-mediated processes. The evidence suggests that Ammonium Lactate may be associated with Granuloma Annulare, a condition that can lead to Contact Dermatitis. Both Granuloma Annulare and Contact Dermatitis are linked to inflammatory responses, with Desoximetasone and Prednicarbate, which are corticosteroids, acting as potential mediators in the progression of these conditions. The presence of these corticosteroids in the pathway indicates a possible role in modulating inflammation, which could contribute to the development of Contact Dermatitis.\n\nThe evidence also highlights a potential link between Ammonium Lactate and Exanthem, a broader category of skin reactions, which in turn may be associated with Benzoyl Peroxide, another agent that can induce Contact Dermatitis. While the exact mechanism by which Ammonium Lactate influences these conditions remains unclear, the observed pathways suggest a role in inflammatory regulation, possibly through immune modulation or the suppression of inflammatory mediators.\n\nThe integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Ammonium Lactate may contribute to the development of Contact Dermatitis by influencing inflammatory processes, though further mechanistic studies are needed to elucidate the precise biological interactions involved. The evidence is consistent with a role in immune-mediated inflammation, but the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully characterized.",
